This question resolves **YES** if, between 1 January 2026 and 31 December 2026 (UTC), any of the following entities publishes a Sudan-war total **excess-mortality estimate (direct + indirect)** with a **central / point estimate exceeding 200,000 deaths**, applied at **nationwide** scope (i.e., covering "Sudan", "the Sudan war", or "the conflict" - not a single state, single city, or single event):

1. A peer-reviewed article in a PubMed-indexed medical or epidemiological journal (the *Lancet* family, *BMJ* family, *NEJM*, *JAMA*, *PLOS*, *Nature*, *Science*, *Conflict and Health*, or similar);
2. A working paper or preprint by a named academic research group with a university affiliation, hosted on SSRN, medRxiv, ResearchGate, or an institutional site (e.g., LSHTM Sudan Research Group, Yale Humanitarian Research Lab, UCLouvain CRED);
3. A formal publication of a UN body (UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for Sudan, OHCHR, OCHA, WHO, UNICEF) presenting the figure as the body's own estimate, not a citation of others;
4. A formal report or press release with stated methodology from a major international NGO with epidemiological capacity (MSF, ICRC, IRC, Save the Children, or similar);
5. A national government report (US State Department, UK FCDO, EU EUAA, or similar) presenting its own modelled figure (not a citation);
6. A documented release or revision of the [CRED EM-DAT](https://www.emdat.be/) or [Uppsala UCDP](https://ucdp.uu.se/) database that lists a Sudan-war cumulative total exceeding the threshold, accompanied by a public report or release note.

This question resolves **NO** if no such publication appears by 31 December 2026 (UTC).

questions.background:
On November 5, 2024, Missouri voters approved [Amendment 3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Missouri_Amendment_3) (the "Right to Reproductive Freedom Initiative") by roughly 52% to 48%, adding Article I, Section 36 to the state constitution and establishing a fundamental right to reproductive freedom, including abortion through fetal viability. Missouri was the first state to roll back a pre-Dobbs near-total abortion ban via citizen initiative.

In May 2025, the Missouri General Assembly [passed House Joint Resolution 73](https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/planned-parenthood-great-plains-votes/press-releases/anti-abortion-lawmakers-in-the-missouri-house-approve-measure-to-overturn-the-will-of-the-voters-and-ban-abortion), referring a new constitutional amendment to the November 3, 2026 ballot. If approved, the 2026 Amendment 3 would:

* Repeal Article I, Section 36 (the 2024 amendment);
* Prohibit abortion except in cases of medical emergency, fetal anomaly, or rape and incest (the latter only up to 12 weeks' gestation);
* Require parental or guardian consent for minors seeking abortion;
* Prohibit gender-affirming medical care for minors (already barred under SB 49 until August 2027).

The "Yes on 3" campaign [is led](https://missouriindependent.com/2026/03/30/missouri-abortion-amendment-3-campaign-effort-2026/) by the Her Health, Her Future PAC, with Missouri First Lady Claudia Kehoe as treasurer and backing from Governor Mike Kehoe and Republican legislative leadership. The opposition campaign, Stop the Ban, includes Planned Parenthood, the ACLU of Missouri, and Abortion Action Missouri.

Missouri constitutional amendments referred by the legislature require a simple statewide majority of votes cast on the measure to pass.

On June 30, 2026, NPR [retracted](https://www.npr.org/2026/06/30/nx-s1-4622951/samuel-alito-retires) a story reporting that Samuel Alito, age 76, was retiring. There has been similar [speculation](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/supreme-court-justices-alito-thomas-not-retiring-sources-say/) about Clarence Thomas, 78, retiring. Amy Coney Barrett, though only 54, has faced [calls](https://www.dailymail.com/news/article-15942533/trump-amy-coney-barrett-birthright-citizenship.html) from conservative media to resign due to her opinions on recent cases such as birthright citizenship and whether the executive branch of the federal government can overrulse state laws on mail-in balloting.&#x20;

Supreme Court justices serve a [life tenure](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/life-tenure-us-supreme-court-justices-global-oddity-clear-costs) and are confirmed by a simple majority of the Senate. Thus, if Democrats were to take control of the Senate in the [2026 elections,](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_elections) President Donald Trump would need Democratic senators to approve of any of his Supreme Court nominations.&#x20;

At the time of this question, Republicans control the U.S. Senate 53-47, a  majority of 6 seats. However, Metaculus [assigns](https://www.metaculus.com/questions/36370/us-senate-plurality-after-2026-midterms/) Republicans only a 55% chance of retaining the chamber. Republicans face a difficult [map](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-election/) in the 2026 cycle, defending contested seats in North Carolina, Georgia, Ohio, Maine, and perhaps Texas and Iowa. Democrats  are defending New Hampshire and Michigan. Historically, the out-of-power party often has an advantage in midterm elections. (See, e.g., the Brookings analysis [What history tells us about the 2026 midterm elections](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-history-tells-us-about-the-2026-midterm-elections/).)

On April 28, 2026, the United Arab Emirates [stated](https://www.reuters.com/markets/commodities/uae-says-it-quits-opec-opec-statement-2026-04-28/) it was quitting the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) due to the unprecedented energy crisis caused by [the Iran war](https://www.reuters.com/world/iran/).

> In his first public comments since the announcement, UAE Energy Minister Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei told Reuters in ​a telephone interview that the decision was taken after examining the country's energy strategies.
> &#x20;
> He said the UAE had not discussed the issue with any other country.
> &#x20;
> "This ​is a policy decision, it has been done after a careful look at current and future policies related to level of production," ⁠Mazrouei said.
> &#x20;
> …
> &#x20;
> He also said the world would demand more energy, implying the UAE would be positioned to meet that need.
> &#x20;
> Oil prices on international markets [trimmed ​gains](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/oil-prices-rise-no-end-iran-war-stand-off-seems-sight-2026-04-28/) following the UAE's announcement it would on May 1 leave OPEC and OPEC+, which brings together OPEC and allied producers.

The UAE is one of the group's [biggest OPEC+ producers](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/what-is-opec-how-does-it-affect-oil-prices-2026-04-28/) and weakens ‌OPEC's control over global oil supplies.

> OPEC was founded in 1960 in Baghdad by Iraq, ‌Iran, Kuwait, Venezuela and Saudi Arabia with the aims of coordinating petroleum policies and securing fair and stable prices. \[In April, it included] 12 countries, mainly from the Middle East. The UAE joined in 1967.
> &#x20;
> The UAE is the fourth producer to leave the group in recent years, and by far the biggest. Angola, which joined OPEC in 2007, quit the bloc at the start of 2024, citing disagreements over production levels. Ecuador quit ​OPEC in 2020 and Qatar in 2019.

